{"name":"Light Pink Polar","hex":"#d8c9cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"},{"term":"polar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or having a pole or polarity, as:\n Of, relating to, measured from, or referred to a geographic pole (the North Pole or South Pole); within the Arctic or Antarctic circles.\n\n Of an orbit that passes over, or near, one of these poles.\n Having a dipole; ionic.\n Of a coordinate system, specifying the location of a point in a plane by using a radius and an angle.\n Having but two possible answers, yes and no.\n Of or relating to a pole (extreme) on any spectrum or field, such as an ideologically pure dogmatic position as opposed to any syncretic integration or balancing of competing principles.","Of, relating to, measured from, or referred to a geographic pole (the North Pole or South Pole); within the Arctic or Antarctic circles."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The line joining the points of contact of tangents drawn to meet a curve from a point called the pole of the line."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polar"}],"entries":[{"title":"X11 color names","blocks":[{"text":"In computing, on the X Window System, X11 color names are represented in a simple text file, which maps certain strings to RGB color values. It was traditionally shipped with every X11 installation, hence the name, and is usually located in <X11root>/lib/X11/rgb.txt. The web colors list is descended from it but differs for certain color names."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X11_color_names","wikidata":"Q376249"},{"title":"Shades of pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink colors are usually light or desaturated shades of reds, roses, and magentas which are created on computer and television screens using the RGB color model and in printing with the CMYK color model. As such, it is an arbitrary classification of color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Color_icon_pink_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_pink_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_pink","wikidata":"Q19847189"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShades_of_red.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Globe_amaranth.jpg/500px-Globe_amaranth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Globe_amaranth.jpg/500px-Globe_amaranth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pink globe amaranth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlobe_amaranth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg/500px-Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg/1280px-Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg/500px-Row_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cherry blossoms blooming in the spring in Japan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARow_of_sakura%2C_Asuwa_River%2C_Fukui.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.